      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm running into a major problem with the harmonic interference patterns in my latest session. i've been using a series of oscillating sub-bass frequencies paired with specific dissonant major-seventh clusters to test neurological entrainment, and the results are actually working too well. \n\nwhen i hit the 4.5Hz - 7Hz range (theta band) while layering those specific dissonant intervals, i've noticed that the people in the room lose all sense of agency—it's like their individual willpower just flattens out into a single, unified wave. it's not just relaxation; it's a complete phase-shift in their decision-making capability. \n\ni need to map the mathematical relationship between the amplitude of the dissonant cluster and the \"agency coefficient\" of the subjects. if i can't calculate the exact threshold where the dissonance overrides personal volition, i might accidentally create a standing wave that's impossible to break once it starts. how would i model the decay rate of human agency when subjected to these specific acoustic interference patterns?",
